00:14:19,670 --> 00:14:22,290
are needed for energy
production by the bacteria.

242
00:14:23,170 --> 00:14:26,470
Now, in terms of
virulence, it's similar to

243
00:14:26,470 --> 00:14:30,450
strep pneumonia in that
it secretes IgA protease,

244
00:14:30,450 --> 00:14:33,090
and that's what allows
it to reside in the

245
00:14:33,090 --> 00:14:36,850
respiratory mucosa. It
also produces a capsule.

246
00:14:37,090 --> 00:14:40,730
So Haemophilus influenzae
is then going to be

247
00:14:40,730 --> 00:14:44,210
divided into six antigenically
distinct capsular

248
00:14:44,210 --> 00:14:47,570
serotypes, which are going
to be labeled A through F.

249
00:14:47,910 --> 00:14:52,550
Five of those serotypes,
all but B, have capsules

250
00:14:52,550 --> 00:14:54,970
that are composed of
different hexo-sugars

251
00:14:54,970 --> 00:14:59,470
rather than the pentose
-based PRP seen in type B.

252
00:15:00,030 --> 00:15:04,010
Now, again, similar
to strep pneumoniae,

253
00:15:04,150 --> 00:15:08,430
Haemophilus influenzae
only infects humans and is

254
00:15:08,430 --> 00:15:11,110
also a natural colonizer
of the upper respiratory

255
00:15:11,110 --> 00:15:15,230
tract. And therefore, it can
be spread person to person

256
00:15:15,230 --> 00:15:19,370
through droplets or other
respiratory secretions.

257
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:27,280
Haemophilus causes a number
of different diseases,

258
00:15:27,340 --> 00:15:29,860
and you could use the
four letters in the genus

259
00:15:29,860 --> 00:15:32,400
name to remember them,
like you can see here.

260
00:15:32,780 --> 00:15:37,360
The first is epiglutitis,
which is classically identified

261
00:15:37,360 --> 00:15:41,140
as having a cherry red
appearance or seen as a

262
00:15:41,140 --> 00:15:44,560
thumb sign on an x-ray, like
you can see in the image.

263
00:15:45,200 --> 00:15:49,020
This is now fairly
uncommon due to the Hib

264
00:15:49,020 --> 00:15:52,530
vaccine, and that's also
a similar situation for

265
00:15:52,530 --> 00:15:55,440
meningitis, where
Haemophilus used to be the

266
00:15:55,440 --> 00:15:58,120
leading cause before
vaccination was common.

267
00:15:58,940 --> 00:16:02,120
Haemophilus will also
cause ear infections,

268
00:16:02,120 --> 00:16:05,100
and it can cause
pneumonia in adults,

269
00:16:05,100 --> 00:16:07,260
which is why we're
discussing it here.

270
00:16:07,260 --> 00:16:09,700
You might also see
it associated with

271
00:16:09,700 --> 00:16:12,240
sepsis and
conjunctivitis as well.

272
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:20,360
Klebsiella pneumoniae is a less

273
00:16:20,360 --> 00:16:21,920
frequent cause of pneumonia.

274
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:26,400
It's a gram-negative enteric
bacillus that ferments

275
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:29,660
lactose. So in a lab, you
would use different types

276
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:33,480
of media that indicate
fermentation to help identify

277
00:16:33,480 --> 00:16:37,620
Klebsiella. So for
instance in the image it's

278
00:16:37,620 --> 00:16:40,780
growing on a McConkie agar
plate which is designed to

279
00:16:40,780 --> 00:16:44,140
isolate enteric bacteria
and then differentiate them

280
00:16:44,140 --> 00:16:46,840
using the characteristic
of lactose fermentation.

281
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:51,980
Klebsiella pneumoniae is
a normal inhabitant of the

282
00:16:51,980 --> 00:16:55,620
GI tract but it can also be
carried in the respiratory

283
00:16:55,620 --> 00:16:58,100
tract of about 10 percent
of healthy people.

284
00:16:58,710 --> 00:17:02,960
It can also be aspirated
into the lungs and can act

285
00:17:02,960 --> 00:17:07,180
as a primary pathogen due
to the presence of a capsule

286
00:17:07,180 --> 00:17:11,280
which is one of its main
virulence factors now it's

287
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:14,920
that very large capsule
that also gives the colonies

288
00:17:14,920 --> 00:17:18,440
growing on a plate that
very mucoid appearance

289
00:17:18,440 --> 00:17:22,080
that you can see in the image
and it's also what produces

290
00:17:22,080 --> 00:17:25,320
the thick mucoid sputum
in patients with the

291
00:17:25,320 --> 00:17:29,500
infection which is referred
to as current jelly sputum.

292
00:17:30,260 --> 00:17:32,180
Infections can also progress to

293
00:17:32,180 --> 00:17:34,480
necrosis and abscess formation.

294
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:37,240
And of course, being
a gram-negative

295
00:17:37,240 --> 00:17:40,140
species, it also has
lipid A as a structural

296
00:17:40,140 --> 00:17:43,100
component of its LPS
layer, which can act

297
00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:45,720
as an endotoxin if
dead cells are lysed.

298
00:17:46,060 --> 00:17:49,920
Now over on the side,
you can see the key fact

299
00:17:49,920 --> 00:17:54,080
mnemonic related to Klebsiella.
We know the bacteria

300
00:17:54,080 --> 00:17:56,760
can aspirate into the
respiratory tract and

301
00:17:56,760 --> 00:17:59,660
that it's associated with
alcohol abuse as a risk

302
00:17:59,660 --> 00:18:03,340
factor, as well as being
associated with diabetic

303
00:18:03,340 --> 00:18:05,960
patients that have an
impaired immune response.

304
00:18:11,770 --> 00:18:14,490
Okay, we'll move to
Bordetella pertussis,

305
00:18:14,490 --> 00:18:18,490
which is another human
-only pathogen that's

306
00:18:18,490 --> 00:18:21,270
easily transmitted by
respiratory droplets

307
00:18:21,270 --> 00:18:24,610
and is also the cause
of whooping cough, which

308
00:18:24,610 --> 00:18:26,610
is going to generally
be seen in infants.

309
00:18:26,850 --> 00:18:29,850
Now, we're discussing
it here with pneumonia

310
00:18:29,850 --> 00:18:33,090
because secondary
bacterial pneumonia is

311
00:18:33,090 --> 00:18:35,710
the most common
complication and also the

312
00:18:35,710 --> 00:18:38,110
leading cause of death
in pertussis cases.

313
00:18:39,910 --> 00:18:44,390
Bordadella is a gram
-negative cacobacillus that is

314
00:18:44,390 --> 00:18:47,730
fastidious. So if you want
to culture it in a lab, you

315
00:18:47,730 --> 00:18:51,070
would need a specialized
medium called Bordage and

316
00:18:51,070 --> 00:18:55,710
goo that has a very high
percentage of blood in it.

317
00:18:55,950 --> 00:18:59,870
Now, in terms of infection,
the organism will

318
00:18:59,870 --> 00:19:02,790
attach to the ciliated
epithelium of the upper

319
00:19:02,790 --> 00:19:06,970
respiratory tract, but
doesn't typically invade any

320
00:19:06,970 --> 00:19:09,270
further than that into
the underlying tissue.

321
00:19:09,500 --> 00:19:12,570
And the attachment is actually
part of the virulence,

322
00:19:12,570 --> 00:19:16,530
where the bacterium can
use fimbriae along with

323
00:19:16,530 --> 00:19:19,270
a pillus-like protein
called filamentous

324
00:19:19,270 --> 00:19:22,910
hemagglutinin that allows it
to anchor to the host cell.

325
00:19:23,090 --> 00:19:25,470
But the pathogenesis
is really due

326
00:19:25,470 --> 00:19:27,490
to the toxins that it produces.

327
00:19:27,930 --> 00:19:32,110
So the first toxin is
pertussis toxin, and this

328
00:19:32,110 --> 00:19:35,870
is going to disrupt
internal cellular signaling.

329
00:19:36,210 --> 00:19:39,830
It does that by
increasing the activity of

330
00:19:39,830 --> 00:19:42,410
an enzyme known as
adenylate cyclase, and

331
00:19:42,410 --> 00:19:44,730
that is going to
increase the amount of

332
00:19:44,730 --> 00:19:46,970
a signaling molecule
known as cyclic AMP.

333
00:19:47,450 --> 00:19:50,630
ultimately that leads to
edema in the respiratory

334
00:19:50,630 --> 00:19:54,910
mucosa so by altering the
cellular communication

335
00:19:54,910 --> 00:20:00,290
it causes fluid to seep
into the airways a toxin

336
00:20:00,290 --> 00:20:04,770
also will lead to
lymphocytosis which is a higher

337
00:20:04,770 --> 00:20:07,950
than normal amount of
lymphocytes in the bloodstream

338
00:20:07,950 --> 00:20:11,930
so it actually impairs
signaling within lymphocytes

339
00:20:11,930 --> 00:20:14,630
as well so that they
can no longer enter into

340
00:20:14,630 --> 00:20:17,570
the lymphoid tissue or
exit from the bloodstream.

341
00:20:18,630 --> 00:20:23,750
The second toxin is
adenylate cyclate toxin, and

342
00:20:23,750 --> 00:20:28,490
that can directly convert
intracellular ATP to cyclic

343
00:20:28,490 --> 00:20:32,270
AMP as a signaling molecule.
And if that happens

344
00:20:32,270 --> 00:20:35,390
in phagocytes like macrophages
and neutrophils, it

345
00:20:35,390 --> 00:20:39,170
basically will paralyze
those phagocytes and prevent

346
00:20:39,170 --> 00:20:41,790
them from engulfing and
clearing the bacteria.

347
00:20:42,490 --> 00:20:46,730
The third toxin is called
tracheal cytotoxin and

348
00:20:46,730 --> 00:20:49,910
this is actually a fragment
of the cell wall gets

349
00:20:49,910 --> 00:20:53,730
released from the bacteria
which ultimately will destroy

350
00:20:53,730 --> 00:20:57,930
the ciliated epithelial
cells. So that in turn

351
00:20:57,930 --> 00:21:01,810
will impair the functioning
of the mucociliary escalator

352
00:21:01,810 --> 00:21:05,830
which leads to a thick
mucus buildup and then

353
00:21:05,830 --> 00:21:09,490
ultimately the characteristic
violent coughing that's

354
00:21:09,490 --> 00:21:12,190
associated with the second
stage of the disease.

355
00:21:12,970 --> 00:21:17,390
The first stage, the
catarrhal stage, usually

356
00:21:17,390 --> 00:21:19,990
lasts about a week or
two, and that usually

357
00:21:19,990 --> 00:21:23,970
just presents with mild
cold-like symptoms, you

358
00:21:23,970 --> 00:21:26,450
know, like a runny nose
or a low-grade fever,

359
00:21:26,650 --> 00:21:31,350
and then that gives way
to the paroxysmal stage,

360
00:21:31,350 --> 00:21:34,310
and that's the classic
stage where you get

361
00:21:34,310 --> 00:21:38,030
the intense cough followed
by the whoop as air

362
00:21:38,030 --> 00:21:41,430
moves through swollen
and obstructed airways.

363
00:21:41,630 --> 00:21:44,630
Now, the violent coughing
can also lead to vomiting

364
00:21:44,630 --> 00:21:48,950
as well. And then ultimately,
the last stage is the

365
00:21:48,950 --> 00:21:51,930
convalescent stage where
you get gradual recovery.

366
00:21:53,330 --> 00:21:56,490
In terms of treatment,
usually macrolides are going

367
00:21:56,490 --> 00:21:59,720
to be used to kill the
bacteria, but the pathogenesis

368
00:21:59,720 --> 00:22:02,410
is really due to the
toxins. And so supportive

369
00:22:02,410 --> 00:22:06,590
care is usually necessary,
especially for infant

370
00:22:06,590 --> 00:22:10,310
cases. And, of course,
this can be prevented with

371
00:22:10,310 --> 00:22:15,170
either vaccine that will
protect against tetanus,

372
00:22:15,170 --> 00:22:19,090
diphtheria, and pertussis,
but just differ in

373
00:22:19,090 --> 00:22:22,150
dosage and the age groups
that they're designed for.

374
00:22:27,470 --> 00:22:29,670
All right, we'll shift
gears here to the

375
00:22:29,670 --> 00:22:33,090
atypical community
-acquired bacterial species.

376
00:22:33,770 --> 00:22:36,210
Now, as I mentioned earlier,

377
00:22:36,790 --> 00:22:39,890
atypical really has to
do with the fact that

378
00:22:39,890 --> 00:22:43,870
they're just harder to
culture and stain. They tend

379
00:22:43,870 --> 00:22:47,710
to also cause milder
respiratory symptoms, and

380
00:22:47,710 --> 00:22:50,230
sometimes they require
different antibiotics.

381
00:22:51,060 --> 00:22:54,230
So let's start here with
mycoplasma, which is

382
00:22:54,230 --> 00:22:56,870
the most common type
of atypical pneumonia.

383
00:23:02,460 --> 00:23:06,680
Mycoplasma is a unique
group because not

384
00:23:06,680 --> 00:23:08,400
only are they the
smallest free-living

385
00:23:08,400 --> 00:23:11,640
organisms, but they
also have no cell wall,

386
00:23:11,640 --> 00:23:13,710
which is kind of a
bit odd for bacteria.

387
00:23:14,490 --> 00:23:17,840
Now, due to the lack of a
cell wall, they also don't

388
00:23:17,840 --> 00:23:21,010
typically have a uniform
shape and are considered to be

389
00:23:21,010 --> 00:23:25,240
pleomorphic, like you can
see in the image. And that

390
00:23:25,240 --> 00:23:28,200
lack of a cell wall also
means that they won't gram

391
00:23:28,200 --> 00:23:32,200
stain effectively, nor will
they be inhibited by beta

392
00:23:32,200 --> 00:23:35,760
-lactam antibiotics, which
disrupt cell wall synthesis.

393
00:23:36,160 --> 00:23:39,480
Now, to compensate a bit
for this, their outer

394
00:23:39,480 --> 00:23:43,560
surface has a flexible
membrane that contains

395
00:23:43,560 --> 00:23:47,000
cholesterol, which is a
sterol found in eukaryotic

396
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:50,760
membranes rather than
prokaryotic membranes.

397
00:23:51,040 --> 00:23:54,680
Typically, prokaryotic
cells will use molecules

398
00:23:54,680 --> 00:23:58,380
called hopanoids as a
functional analog to sterols.

399
00:23:59,020 --> 00:24:03,660
Now, you can cultivate
mycoplasma very slowly on

400
00:24:03,660 --> 00:24:07,440
specialized agar called
Eaton agar, which produces

401
00:24:07,440 --> 00:24:12,180
kind of tiny granular
colonies that generally will

402
00:24:12,180 --> 00:24:16,100
develop a very dense center
and a thinner periphery

403
00:24:16,100 --> 00:24:19,880
that tend to take on the
appearance of a fried egg.

404
00:24:21,400 --> 00:24:26,160
Mycoplasma pneumoniae is
pathogenic only in humans.

405
00:24:26,200 --> 00:24:30,060
It's transmitted by
respiratory droplets, and

406
00:24:30,060 --> 00:24:32,860
it also causes what's
known as walking pneumonia.

407
00:24:33,340 --> 00:24:36,860
It usually infects
younger people and is

408
00:24:36,860 --> 00:24:39,660
often the cause of
outbreaks of pneumonia

409
00:24:39,660 --> 00:24:42,580
in groups that have
very close contacts.

410
00:24:46,680 --> 00:24:50,560
the onset of symptoms
is usually gradual here

411
00:24:50,560 --> 00:24:54,680
with various constitutional
symptoms and typically

412
00:24:54,680 --> 00:24:58,740
begins with a non-productive
cough the diagnosis

413
00:24:58,740 --> 00:25:02,800
is typically going to
be clinical and if a

414
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:05,920
chest x-ray is taken there
might be a difference

415
00:25:05,920 --> 00:25:08,860
between the infiltrate
seen there versus the

416
00:25:08,860 --> 00:25:11,780
minimal findings typically
seen on examination.

417
00:25:12,620 --> 00:25:14,920
Molecular techniques
like PCR could also

418
00:25:14,920 --> 00:25:18,700
be used, along with
serology like an ELISA.

419
00:25:19,780 --> 00:25:23,830
A cold agglutinin test
is also a way to test for

420
00:25:23,830 --> 00:25:28,320
mycoplasma pneumoniae, but
it's not really used all that

421
00:25:28,320 --> 00:25:32,940
frequently anymore. So the
basis of the test is the

422
00:25:32,940 --> 00:25:37,640
production of igm antibodies
during the infection and those

423
00:25:37,640 --> 00:25:40,840
antibodies will cross react
with red blood cells and

424
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:45,120
cause them to clump or
agglutinate but that only happens

425
00:25:45,120 --> 00:25:48,820
at four degrees rather than
happening at 37 degrees

426
00:25:48,890 --> 00:25:52,580
and you can see this in the
image where the top test

427
00:25:52,580 --> 00:25:56,820
tube shows cold agglutinins
reacting with red blood cells

428
00:25:56,820 --> 00:26:00,920
and clumping because of that
and the bottom test tube

429
00:26:00,920 --> 00:26:03,780
shows a control where there's
no reaction occurring.

430
00:26:05,080 --> 00:26:09,540
The treatment choice for this
will either be a macrolide,

431
00:26:09,630 --> 00:26:13,460
tetracycline, or a
fluoroquinolone, as opposed to

432
00:26:13,460 --> 00:26:16,720
a beta-lactam because there's
no cell wall to disrupt.

433
00:26:16,720 --> 00:26:17,260
Okay,

434
00:26:22,570 --> 00:26:27,050
the genus Chlamydophila is
still frequently referred

435
00:26:27,050 --> 00:26:30,270
to as chlamydia, so you
might see either genus

436
00:26:30,270 --> 00:26:33,570
name used. And this is
true for Chlamydophylla

437
00:26:33,570 --> 00:26:37,990
pneumoniae that we'll cover
here, as well as Chlamydophylla

438
00:26:37,990 --> 00:26:41,850
cytosi, which causes
the condition cytokosis.

439
00:26:41,850 --> 00:26:43,850
It's a disease characterized

440
00:26:43,850 --> 00:26:45,990
also mainly by pneumonia.

441
00:26:47,170 --> 00:26:48,610
Now, there are some unique

442
00:26:48,610 --> 00:26:50,690
features to the
chlamydial group.

443
00:26:50,810 --> 00:26:53,600
We were just talking
about mycoplasma,

444
00:26:53,600 --> 00:26:57,590
which has no cell
wall, so let's stick

445
00:26:57,590 --> 00:26:59,330
with that as the
focus here first.

446
00:26:59,570 --> 00:27:04,480
These guys do have a rigid
cell wall, and that wall

447
00:27:04,480 --> 00:27:08,790
resembles a gram-negative
type, but it contains an

448
00:27:08,790 --> 00:27:13,770
augmented form of peptidoglycan
that has a significantly

449
00:27:13,770 --> 00:27:17,430
reduced amount of the
building block muramic acid.

450
00:27:18,270 --> 00:27:22,130
Now, another unique aspect
to the group is similar

451
00:27:22,130 --> 00:27:25,410
to the rickettsial
bacterial group, insofar as

452
00:27:25,410 --> 00:27:28,430
they are both obligate
intracellular organisms.

453
00:27:28,930 --> 00:27:32,850
So neither one can generate
enough of their own

454
00:27:32,850 --> 00:27:36,050
ATP to survive and
replicate, and so they rely

455
00:27:36,050 --> 00:27:39,730
on the host cell instead
as an energy parasite.

456
00:27:41,430 --> 00:27:46,290
Chlamydial species are
typically seen in two different

457
00:27:46,290 --> 00:27:51,230
forms. So there is a spore
-like and inert elementary

458
00:27:51,230 --> 00:27:54,990
body, which is infectious
to cells, and then there

459
00:27:54,990 --> 00:27:59,190
is a metabolically active
reticulate body that will

460
00:27:59,190 --> 00:28:03,350
undergo division and produce
cytoplasmic inclusions

461
00:28:03,350 --> 00:28:08,570
within an infected cell. And
that's true of many of the

462
00:28:08,570 --> 00:28:14,090
chlamydial species. Now,
Chlamidophylla pneumoniae is

463
00:28:14,090 --> 00:28:17,830
a species that typically
only infects humans and is

464
00:28:17,830 --> 00:28:20,830
going to be transmitted
person-to-person by aerosol.

465
00:28:21,650 --> 00:28:25,850
To be thorough here,
Chlamidophylla cytosi also

466
00:28:25,850 --> 00:28:30,150
infects birds, and human
infection will usually occur by

467
00:28:30,150 --> 00:28:33,870
inhaling organisms from
dried airborne bird feces.

468
00:28:34,670 --> 00:28:39,530
now since the cells
are too small and are

469
00:28:39,530 --> 00:28:42,990
intracellular typically
gram staining is usually not

470
00:28:42,990 --> 00:28:45,990
all that effective so
diagnosis could be done

471
00:28:45,990 --> 00:28:49,030
through game sustaining
and then visualizing

472
00:28:49,030 --> 00:28:53,830
inclusions within cells under
a microscope but certainly

473
00:28:53,830 --> 00:28:56,970
molecular tests like
PCR are going to be a

474
00:28:56,970 --> 00:29:00,280
more sensitive and faster
way to diagnose and

475
00:29:00,280 --> 00:29:03,470
serological tests like
an Liza can be used also.

476
00:29:06,890 --> 00:29:10,590
All right, so our last
atypical pathogen is

477
00:29:10,590 --> 00:29:14,070
Legionella pneumophila, and
this brings us back to a

478
00:29:14,070 --> 00:29:17,070
species that has a true
cell wall, which happens

479
00:29:17,070 --> 00:29:20,730
to be a gram-negative type
